WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.890
It's Thursday, November 12, and this is the Singularity Daily.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.010
Here's your day in superintelligence, in 3 minutes flat.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.280
Today's about proof.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:21.730
A Nobel winner's team says OpenAI's AI solved half a million-dollar physics problem.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.090
Their honest verdict? "We do not claim to fully understand the proof yet."

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.950
When Trump and Xi meet next week,

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:38.110
America says they'll advance a new dialogue on superintelligence.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.280
Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent wants to keep "hu

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.240
man judgment firmly in command of this technology."

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:51.040
This week OpenAI unveiled a kill switch for its research models.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.270
Stop time? 35 seconds, max.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.090
Google staff are reportedly testing a fast new Gemini,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.470
code-named Hydrogen. Fitting name.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.160
Now, the money.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.650
Anthropic's bankers are reportedly weighing a $1.5 trillion price tag.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.890
OpenAI reportedly hit $10 billion in quarterly revenue, and lost even more.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.650
CoreWeave, which rents out computing power for AI,

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:21.340
jumped over 20% and sparked a rally.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.360
And British chip startup Fractile is now worth $7 billion,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.560
with the UK government on board.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.550
Google DeepMind's AlphaGenome 2 predicts how a change in your DNA plays out,

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:35.390
cell by cell.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.190
It spots nearly twice as many DNA changes that affect genes,

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.460
and researchers can use it for free.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.210
The catch? A senator now wants Nvidia's boss,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.350
Jensen Huang, to quit over the spy scandal.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.770
In England, students are passing some uni assessments in minutes, with AI.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.370
So... is it so over?

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.450
We're so back!

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.010
Serve Robotics just opened its 2,000 sidewalk delivery robots to anyone,

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.150
even AI agents.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.660
Their pitch? "Why does getting something delivered from China cost $1,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.480
but from Chinatown cost $10?"

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.380
And SpaceX is reportedly designing its own AI racks,

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.760
for data centers here and in orbit.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.080
Machines can do the work now. Proving it,

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.650
and understanding it, is still up to us.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.240
Zoom out, and the line only goes up.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.540
And the future keeps arriving ahead of schedule.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.880
One day, all of this. Tomorrow, probably more.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.200
You've been watching the Singularity Daily.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:49.940
See you tomorrow.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.010
Want the whole month? Hear more on November 27.
